  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Ohio has recorded $257M in federal contract obligations so far in FY2027 to date. The state received 1,068 awards with an average award size of $240K. Obligations fell 96.9% year over year. Historical context shows large swings: Ohio obligations were $7.8B in FY22, $10.3B in FY23, $9.4B in FY24, $10B in FY25, and $8.4B in FY26. The Department of Veterans Affairs and the Department of Energy are the largest buyers in the current fiscal year, with $102M and $63.7M respectively. Southern Ohio Cleanup Company LLC is the top vendor with $63.3M in obligations, and Remediation Services is the largest industry by obligations at $63.8M. Other notable buyers include the General Services Administration with $29.6M and the Department of Health and Human Services with $18.5M. Commercial and Institutional Building Construction accounts for $44.8M in obligations and several health, engineering, and research industries appear in the top ten industries. The current-year totals are partial and represent fiscal year to date obligations.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How concentrated is Ohio federal contracting right now?",
        "body": "The Department of Veterans Affairs and the Department of Energy together account for a large share of FY2027 to date obligations, with $102M and $63.7M respectively. The top vendor, SOUTHERN OHIO CLEANUP COMPANY LLC, has $63.3M in obligations, and the top industry, Remediation Services, has $63.8M. Total obligations for Ohio are $257M across 1,068 awards, so a small set of buyers and vendors is driving a big portion of current spending. Current-year totals are fiscal year to date and therefore partial."
      },
      {
        "heading": "What industries and vendors are driving the FY2027 to date totals?",
        "body": "Remediation Services and Commercial and Institutional Building Construction lead industries with $63.8M and $44.8M respectively. SOUTHERN OHIO CLEANUP COMPANY LLC, LC FLUHARTY CONSTRUCTION LLC, and VETERANS CONTRACTING, INC. are the top three vendors with $63.3M, $14.9M, and $14M respectively. Several health, engineering, and energy-related firms and NAICS activities appear in the top ten industries and vendors, reflecting a mix of cleanup, construction, and technical services in current obligations."
      }
    ]
